Say where a software-decoded video's dropped frames come from, on OHOS.

YouTube's 1080p60 HDR (VP9 profile 2, decoded in software) dropped a third
of its frames on a Mate 70 Pro+ while ohos-vp9-row-threads.patch's log had
only an eighth of them decoded late, and a deeper frame queue
(ohos-video-queue-depth.patch) changed nothing. So most frames are lost
after decoding, between the queue and the screen.

Two logs, both OHOS only:

- "OHOS video render", every five seconds, following each frame from the
  decoder to the screen: VideoRendererImpl::Render() calls (once per
  display begin-frame), the longest gap between them, frames dropped, and
  how many frames were queued (the current one included), and the interval
  each call was asked to cover (the begin-frame's display interval) beside
  the average real gap between calls; how long each
  Read() of the decoder stream took to come back and how long the renderer
  waited after a frame before the next Read(); and, in FrameReady(), how
  far ahead of its display time each frame arrived, with how many arrived
  late or with less than a frame's time to spare. An earlier version
  counted calls with no effective frame queued, which the current frame
  hides; with the upload made fast by ohos-video-compositor-qos.patch a
  third of the frames still dropped with that count at zero.
- "OHOS video upload", every 300 frames from
  VideoResourceUpdater::CreateForSoftwareFrame(): the frame's size and bit
  depth, the texture format it goes to, and the average and longest time to
  convert and upload it, with how many took over 8 ms. That work runs on the
  same thread as Render().

Generated against Chromium 154.0.8037.51.

Apply with:
  git -C /path/to/chromium/src apply /path/to/patches/ohos-video-render-diagnostics.patch

